<p>{% person "elijah-boley" %}Elijah Boley{% endperson %} and his son {% person "alphonse-boley" %}Alphonse{% endperson %} came to Mandan in 1877. Their arrival caused quite a stir because their wagon was pulled by horses rather than oxen. The Boleys were instrumental in developing horse racing and the events that evolved into Mandan's rodeo.</p>

<p>Promoted as "Older than the Oldest Rodeo," Mandan's rodeo began with competitions held alongside the community fair first held on July 5, 1879. Events included pony races, a baseball game, and demonstrations of area ranchers' roping skills. An 1882 newspaper article was the first publication to formally identify a rodeo as part of the event. In some years, horse racing was the only animal-related competition mentioned in newspaper accounts. During the early 1890s, the day's highlights were the parade and baseball game. Children's games and shooting contests were also frequently mentioned.</p>

<p>On April 25, 1885, the Dakota Territorial Legislature approved an act, signed by Governor {% person "gilbert-a-pierce" %}Pierce{% endperson %}, creating a Board of Agriculture to advance agriculture, horticulture, manufacturing, and domestic arts. Its principal function was to organize an Annual Territorial Fair exhibiting the territory's agriculture, livestock, and other products. The act did not designate a location, prompting keen competition among the territory's larger towns. Mandan began hosting North Dakota's State Fair in 1897 and continued through 1905, supported by $1,250 from the State Legislature. During the 1920s, four North Dakota cities claimed to host the State Fair, including Minot, which held its first event in 1922. After the Mandan event lost its official State Fair designation, annual celebrations continued at the fairgrounds as the Missouri Slope Fair until 1932.</p>

