  </figure>
</div>

<p>Plans were made for a fall fair that year at the Heart River fairgrounds in southwest Mandan, between the river and Main Street. Members of the Boston Syndicate reportedly donated the property with the stipulation that a fair be held each year. The site was enclosed, and an oval track was built with clay laid over sand. Sixteen volunteer teams completed the work, with $400 allotted for the track. Subscriptions totaling $2,225 were collected.</p>

<p>Invitations were sent to area clergymen, the North Dakota Stock Growers Association, and the Wool Growers Association. Tags for grain exhibits were mailed to farmers. The Dickinson band was hired, and North Dakota Governor {% person "roger-allin" %}Allin{% endperson %} was the guest speaker at the opening-day ceremonies. Approximately two dozen Mandan residents served as superintendents of exhibit departments. Scores of prizes were awarded for grain and vegetable exhibits; horses, cattle, and swine; horse races; and entries in the women's department, including jellies, cakes, crocheting, handiwork, carpet weaving, paintings, decorated chinaware, and flowers. A baby department awarded prizes for such distinctions as the heaviest baby, prettiest daughter, prettiest hair and eyes, best-natured baby, sweetest smile, and best all-around baby.</p>

